#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Generate ~130 additional tech-support training examples – batch 11."""
import json, pathlib

examples = [
    ("How do I reset a Windows PC to factory settings without losing the recovery partition?",
     "Use the built-in Reset feature which preserves the recovery partition: Settings → System → Recovery → Reset this PC. Choose 'Remove everything' for a clean slate or 'Keep my files' to preserve user data while reinstalling Windows. Select 'Local reinstall' (uses the recovery image on disk) or 'Cloud download' (downloads a fresh copy from Microsoft—better if the local image might be corrupted). Under 'Additional settings', enable 'Clean data' if you're giving the machine to someone else—this does a secure wipe that takes longer but prevents data recovery. For enterprise devices managed by Autopilot, the device will re-enroll automatically after reset. Important: BitLocker recovery key will be needed if the drive is encrypted and the user isn't signed into their Microsoft account. Back up the key from Azure AD/Entra before resetting."),
    ("What is Windows Sandbox and how is it useful for IT support?",
     "Windows Sandbox is a lightweight, disposable virtual desktop environment built into Windows 10/11 Pro and Enterprise. Enable it: Settings → Apps → Optional Features → More Windows Features → Windows Sandbox. It creates an isolated Windows instance that starts clean every time—when you close it, everything inside is permanently deleted. IT support uses: 1) Testing suspicious email attachments or downloads in a safe environment. 2) Testing software installations before deploying to users. 3) Running potentially unstable applications without risking the host. 4) Testing GPO or registry changes safely. 5) Browsing untrusted websites. It requires: virtualization enabled in BIOS, 4GB+ RAM, 1GB free disk. It's faster than spinning up a full VM because it shares the host's kernel and dynamically generates a clean Windows image. Configuration files (.wsb) let you customize: shared folders, networking, startup scripts, and memory allocation."),
    ("How do I configure a DHCP reservation for a network printer?",
     "DHCP reservations ensure a device always gets the same IP address without static configuration on the device itself. On Windows DHCP Server: open DHCP management console → expand the scope → right-click Reservations → New Reservation. Enter: reservation name (e.g., 'HP-Floor2-Printer'), IP address (pick one from your scope range), MAC address (found on the printer's network configuration page or label—format XX-XX-XX-XX-XX-XX). On a router/firewall: find DHCP settings → Static DHCP or Address Reservation. Same info needed. On Linux (ISC DHCP): edit /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf, add: 'host printer1 { hardware ethernet AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF; fixed-address 192.168.1.50; }', then restart the DHCP service. Best practices: document all reservations in a spreadsheet or IPAM tool. Use a consistent IP range for reservations (e.g., .1-.49 for infrastructure, .50-.99 for printers). After creating the reservation, release and renew the printer's DHCP lease so it picks up the reserved address."),
    ("What is the difference between TCP and UDP, and when is each used?",
     "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is connection-oriented: it establishes a three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer. It guarantees delivery, ordering, and error checking. If a packet is lost, TCP retransmits it. Used for: web browsing (HTTP/HTTPS port 80/443), email (SMTP 25/587, IMAP 143/993, POP3 110/995), file transfers (FTP 21, SMB 445), SSH (22), RDP (3389), database connections. UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is connectionless: no handshake, no delivery guarantee, no ordering. It's faster and lower overhead because there's no connection management. Used for: DNS queries (53), DHCP (67/68), VoIP and video calls, streaming media, online gaming, SNMP (161/162), NTP (123), TFTP (69). Key insight for troubleshooting: if an application uses TCP and it's slow, packet loss causes retransmissions and delays. If it uses UDP and there's packet loss, you get quality degradation (choppy audio, pixelated video) rather than slowness. Some protocols use both: DNS starts with UDP but falls back to TCP for large responses over 512 bytes."),
    ("How do I create a bootable USB drive for Windows installation?",
     "Several methods: 1) Microsoft Media Creation Tool (recommended): download from microsoft.com, run it, select 'Create installation media', choose language, edition, and architecture (64-bit), select 'USB flash drive' (8GB minimum). It downloads Windows and creates the bootable USB automatically. 2) Rufus (more options): download Rufus, select the USB drive, select the Windows ISO, partition scheme (GPT for UEFI, MBR for legacy BIOS), file system (NTFS for Windows), click Start. Rufus also supports creating Windows To Go drives. 3) Command line (UEFI only): 'diskpart' → 'list disk' → 'select disk X' → 'clean' → 'create partition primary' → 'format fs=fat32 quick' → 'assign' → 'exit'. Then copy all files from the mounted ISO to the USB drive. 4) For Linux bootable USB: use Rufus, balenaEtcher, or 'dd if=ubuntu.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress'. Important notes: creating a bootable USB erases all data on the drive. FAT32 format works with UEFI but has a 4GB file limit—install.wim in newer Windows ISOs exceeds this; use Rufus or MCT which handle the split automatically."),
    ("What are Group Policy Preferences and how do they differ from Group Policy Settings?",
     "Group Policy Settings (the traditional GPO settings) enforce a configuration—the user cannot change it, and it reverts when the GPO no longer applies. They modify the policies section of the registry (HKLM/HKCU\\Software\\Policies). Group Policy Preferences (GPP) configure a default that the user CAN change. They modify the actual registry values, map drives, create shortcuts, configure printers, manage local users/groups, set environment variables, and more. When the GPO no longer applies, the preference may persist (configurable). Key differences: Settings are enforced and tattooed (or removed when GPO is removed); Preferences are applied as defaults. Preferences offer item-level targeting—apply based on computer name, IP range, OS, security group, battery status, portable computer, or registry value. This is more granular than security filtering. Common GPP uses: mapping network drives (Preferences → Windows Settings → Drive Maps), deploying printers, creating desktop shortcuts, configuring power settings, setting environment variables, managing local administrators. Best practice: use Preferences for user-facing configurations that might need local flexibility, and Settings for security policies that must be enforced."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot a VPN connection that keeps disconnecting?",
     "Systematic approach: 1) Identify the pattern: does it drop after a specific time (idle timeout), during heavy use (bandwidth issue), or randomly (network instability)? 2) Check VPN client logs: most clients (GlobalProtect, Cisco AnyConnect, FortiClient) have diagnostic logs. Look for: DPD (Dead Peer Detection) failures, rekey failures, authentication timeouts. 3) Network stability: run a continuous ping to the VPN gateway ('ping -t vpn.company.com') and to an internet host simultaneously. If both drop, the user's internet is unstable. If only VPN drops, the issue is VPN-specific. 4) MTU issues: VPN encapsulation adds overhead. If MTU is too high, packets get fragmented or dropped. Test with: 'ping -f -l 1400 vpn.company.com'—decrease the size until it succeeds. Set the network adapter MTU: 'netsh interface ipv4 set subinterface \"Wi-Fi\" mtu=1400'. 5) NAT/firewall interference: some firewalls or NAT devices have short UDP session timeouts that kill IKEv2/IPsec tunnels. Enable NAT keepalives in the VPN client. 6) Check for competing VPN software, proxy settings, or antivirus interference. 7) Try split tunnel if available—full tunnel routes all traffic through VPN, which may overload the connection. 8) Update VPN client to the latest version."),
    ("What is WSUS and how does it help manage Windows updates in an enterprise?",
     "Windows Server Update Services (WSUS) is a free Microsoft tool that lets IT control which Windows updates are deployed to computers in the organization. Instead of each PC downloading updates directly from Microsoft (consuming internet bandwidth), WSUS downloads updates once and distributes them internally. Setup: install the WSUS role on a Windows Server. Configure products (Windows 10, Windows 11, Office) and classifications (Security Updates, Critical Updates, Feature Packs) to synchronize. Configure computers via GPO to point to the WSUS server: Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Windows Update → 'Specify intranet Microsoft update service location'. Workflow: WSUS syncs with Microsoft Update on schedule. IT reviews available updates, tests on a pilot group, then approves for deployment to production groups. Computer groups: create groups (Pilot, Workstations, Servers) to stagger deployments. Reporting: WSUS console shows compliance status per computer and update. Limitations: WSUS doesn't manage non-Microsoft updates, the interface is dated, and it requires maintenance (disk cleanup, index rebuilding). Modern alternative: Windows Update for Business + Intune for cloud-native update management with update rings, feature update deferrals, and compliance reporting."),
    ("How do I configure email forwarding in Microsoft 365 Exchange Online?",
     "Multiple methods: 1) Admin Center forwarding: Microsoft 365 Admin Center → Users → select user → Mail → Email forwarding. Toggle on, enter forwarding address, optionally check 'Keep a copy of forwarded messages'. 2) Exchange Admin Center: Recipients → Mailboxes → select user → Mail flow → Email forwarding. 3) PowerShell: 'Set-Mailbox -Identity user@company.com -ForwardingSMTPAddress external@gmail.com -DeliverToMailboxAndForward $true'. The DeliverToMailboxAndForward flag determines whether a copy stays in the original mailbox. 4) User-configured rules: the user can create Outlook rules to forward specific messages. Admins can block this with: Exchange Admin Center → Mail flow → Rules → create a rule to prevent auto-forwarding to external domains. 5) Transport rules (mail flow rules): for organization-wide forwarding policies. Security considerations: auto-forwarding to external domains is a common attack vector—compromised accounts often set up forwarding to exfiltrate data. Disable external forwarding by default: Anti-spam policies → Outbound spam policy → Automatic forwarding = Off. Create exceptions only for approved use cases. Monitor forwarding: run 'Get-Mailbox -ResultSize Unlimited | Where {$_.ForwardingSMTPAddress -ne $null} | Select Name, ForwardingSMTPAddress'."),
    ("What is Active Directory Federation Services (ADFS) and when is it used?",
     "ADFS provides Single Sign-On (SSO) and federated identity between organizations. It allows users to authenticate once with their AD credentials and access applications across organizational boundaries without separate passwords. How it works: 1) User tries to access an application (e.g., Salesforce, a partner portal). 2) The application redirects to the user's ADFS server (the Identity Provider). 3) ADFS authenticates the user against Active Directory. 4) ADFS issues a security token (SAML or WS-Federation) containing claims (user attributes like name, email, group memberships). 5) The token is sent back to the application, which grants access based on the claims. Common use cases: SSO with cloud applications (Salesforce, ServiceNow, custom apps), B2B partnerships where users from one company access another company's apps, and hybrid identity with Azure AD. However, ADFS is being replaced by Azure AD/Entra ID for most scenarios—Azure AD handles SAML, OAuth 2.0, and OpenID Connect natively, is cloud-managed (no on-premises servers to maintain), supports Conditional Access, and provides better MFA integration. Microsoft recommends migrating from ADFS to Azure AD for most workloads. ADFS still has niche uses: on-premises applications that can't be published through Azure AD, specific claim transformation requirements, or organizations that need to keep authentication entirely on-premises."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot 'No Internet, Secured' Wi-Fi issue on Windows?",
     "This status means the device is connected to the Wi-Fi network and the connection is encrypted (secured), but there's no internet access. Troubleshooting: 1) Check other devices on the same network—if all are affected, the issue is the router/ISP. Reboot the router (unplug for 30 seconds). 2) Check IP configuration: 'ipconfig /all'. If the IP is 169.254.x.x (APIPA), the device didn't get a DHCP address. Try: 'ipconfig /release' then 'ipconfig /renew'. 3) Reset network stack: open elevated command prompt, run: 'netsh winsock reset', 'netsh int ip reset', 'ipconfig /flushdns', 'ipconfig /registerdns'. Restart the computer. 4) Check DNS: try 'nslookup google.com'. If it fails, set DNS manually: Settings → Network → Wi-Fi → adapter properties → DNS = 8.8.8.8 and 8.8.4.4. 5) Disable and re-enable the Wi-Fi adapter: Settings → Network → Advanced network settings → Wi-Fi → Disable, wait 10 seconds, Enable. 6) Forget and reconnect to the network: Settings → Network → Wi-Fi → Manage known networks → select network → Forget. Reconnect with the password. 7) Update Wi-Fi driver: Device Manager → Network adapters → Wi-Fi adapter → Update driver. 8) Check if a proxy is configured: Settings → Network → Proxy → ensure 'Automatically detect settings' is on and manual proxy is off. 9) Check firewall or VPN software that might be blocking internet access."),
    ("What is Windows Event Forwarding (WEF) and how do I set it up for centralized log collection?",
     "WEF collects events from multiple Windows computers to a central collector without third-party agents—it uses the built-in Windows Event Collector service and WinRM. Setup: 1) On the Collector: enable the Windows Event Collector service: 'wecutil qc' (configures the service and creates a listener). 2) Create a subscription: Event Viewer → Subscriptions → Create. Choose source computers (individual or from AD groups). Select events to collect (e.g., Security log, Event ID 4624, 4625, 4688). Choose delivery mode: Collector-initiated (collector pulls from sources, simpler but requires permissions) or Source-initiated (sources push to collector, better for large environments). 3) On Source computers: enable WinRM: 'winrm quickconfig'. Add the collector's computer account to the local Event Log Readers group. For source-initiated: configure via GPO: Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Event Forwarding → Configure target Subscription Manager = 'Server=http://collector:5985/wsman/SubscriptionManager/WEC'. Best practices: create subscriptions for high-value events only (logons, account changes, process creation, PowerShell execution). Send collected events to a SIEM for analysis and alerting. WEF is lightweight and agent-less, making it ideal for environments that can't install third-party agents."),
    ("How do I set up a network file share on Windows Server with proper security?",
     "Steps for creating a secure file share: 1) Create the folder: e.g., D:\\Shares\\Finance. 2) Share it: right-click → Properties → Sharing → Advanced Sharing → check 'Share this folder'. Share name: 'Finance$' (the $ makes it a hidden share, not visible when browsing). Share Permissions: set to 'Authenticated Users' = Full Control (we'll use NTFS permissions for actual security—this is best practice). 3) Set NTFS permissions (Security tab): Remove 'Users' group. Add specific groups: 'Finance-ReadWrite' = Modify (allows create, edit, delete but not change permissions). 'Finance-ReadOnly' = Read & Execute. 'IT-Admins' = Full Control. Use 'Replace all child object permission entries' if applying to existing data. 4) Enable Access-Based Enumeration: Server Manager → File and Storage Services → Shares → right-click → Properties → Settings → Enable access-based enumeration. This hides files/folders users don't have permission to see. 5) Enable auditing: Security tab → Advanced → Auditing → add 'Everyone' for 'Delete' and 'Write' actions. 6) Set quotas: File Server Resource Manager → Quotas → Create quota. 7) Deploy via GPO: Group Policy Preferences → Drive Maps → map the share to a drive letter for relevant users."),
    ("How do I use PowerShell remoting to manage multiple servers simultaneously?",
     "PowerShell remoting uses WinRM (Windows Remote Management) to execute commands on remote computers. Setup: 1) Enable on target servers: 'Enable-PSRemoting -Force' (configures WinRM, creates firewall rules, starts the service). 2) For cross-domain or workgroup: add trusted hosts: 'Set-Item WSMan:\\localhost\\Client\\TrustedHosts -Value \"server1,server2\"'. Single session: 'Enter-PSSession -ComputerName server1 -Credential (Get-Credential)' opens an interactive session. One-to-many (most powerful): 'Invoke-Command -ComputerName server1,server2,server3 -ScriptBlock { Get-Service WinRM }'. This runs the command on all three servers simultaneously and returns results tagged with the computer name. Using sessions for efficiency: '$servers = New-PSSession -ComputerName server1,server2,server3'. 'Invoke-Command -Session $servers -ScriptBlock { Get-Process | Sort CPU -Desc | Select -First 5 }'. Sessions stay open so subsequent commands don't re-authenticate. Copy files to remote servers: 'Copy-Item -Path C:\\Scripts\\update.ps1 -ToSession $session -Destination C:\\Scripts\\'. Best practices: use JEA (Just Enough Administration) to restrict what commands remote users can run. Use CredSSP only when needed (for double-hop scenarios). Log all remoting sessions for auditing."),
    ("What is a network packet capture and how do I use Wireshark for troubleshooting?",
     "A packet capture records all network traffic on an interface for analysis. Wireshark is the most popular tool. Basic usage: 1) Install Wireshark. Select the network interface (Ethernet or Wi-Fi). Click the blue shark fin to start capturing. 2) Capture filters (applied during capture to reduce file size): 'host 192.168.1.100' (only traffic to/from this IP), 'port 443' (only HTTPS traffic), 'tcp port 80 and host 10.0.0.5'. 3) Display filters (applied after capture for analysis): 'http' (show only HTTP traffic), 'tcp.flags.syn==1' (show TCP connection attempts), 'dns' (show DNS queries), 'ip.addr==192.168.1.100 && tcp.port==445' (SMB traffic to specific host). 4) Common troubleshooting uses: TCP retransmissions (filter: 'tcp.analysis.retransmission')—indicates packet loss or network congestion. DNS failures (filter: 'dns.flags.rcode != 0'). TLS handshake issues (filter: 'tls.handshake'). Slow response times—check the time delta between request and response. 5) Follow TCP Stream: right-click a packet → Follow → TCP Stream. Shows the full conversation between client and server in readable format. 6) Statistics → Conversations shows the top talkers on the network. 7) For production servers, use tcpdump to capture and Wireshark to analyze: 'tcpdump -i eth0 -w capture.pcap host 192.168.1.100'. Transfer the .pcap file to your workstation for Wireshark analysis."),
    ("How do I set up and manage Windows Firewall via Group Policy for an enterprise?",
     "Use GPO to centrally manage Windows Defender Firewall across the organization: 1) Open Group Policy Management → create a new GPO linked to the target OU. 2) Navigate to: Computer Configuration → Policies → Windows Settings → Security Settings → Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security. 3) Configure each profile (Domain, Private, Public): set Firewall state to On, Inbound connections to Block, Outbound connections to Allow. 4) Create inbound rules: for example, allow RDP only from IT subnet: New Rule → Port → TCP 3389 → Allow → Scope: Remote IP = 10.10.0.0/24. Allow file sharing: Predefined → File and Printer Sharing → select rules. Allow specific application: Program → browse to the .exe path. 5) Create outbound rules if restricting outbound (less common but useful for servers): block all outbound, then allow specific ports (80, 443, 53). 6) Logging: enable in the profile properties → Logging → log dropped packets and successful connections to %systemroot%\\system32\\LogFiles\\Firewall\\pfirewall.log. Set max file size (4096 KB+). 7) Connection security rules: configure IPsec between servers for encrypted internal traffic. Best practice: test in a pilot OU first. Use 'gpresult /H report.html' on a test machine to verify rules applied. Use 'netsh advfirewall show allprofiles' to check status."),
    ("What is the difference between a hot site, warm site, and cold site in disaster recovery?",
     "These are categories of disaster recovery facilities based on readiness and cost: Cold Site is an empty facility with power, cooling, and network connectivity, but no pre-installed equipment or data. After a disaster, you ship and set up hardware, restore data from backups, and configure everything. Recovery time: days to weeks. Cost: lowest (~$5-10K/month). Best for: non-critical systems with long RTO tolerance. Warm Site has pre-installed hardware and network infrastructure, but data is not continuously synchronized—restored from recent backups. Some configuration is needed. Recovery time: hours to a day. Cost: moderate (~$20-50K/month). Best for: important systems with moderate RTO (4-24 hours). Hot Site is a fully operational mirror of your production environment with real-time or near-real-time data replication. Everything is ready to go—just switch over. Recovery time: minutes to hours. Cost: highest (can equal your production costs). Best for: mission-critical systems requiring minimal downtime (RTO < 4 hours). Modern cloud-based DR blurs these lines: Azure Site Recovery or AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery can replicate VMs to another region, providing hot-site capabilities at warm-site costs. You pay only for storage during normal operations and spin up compute only during a DR event."),
    ("How do I configure Windows to join an Azure AD (Entra ID) domain instead of traditional on-premises AD?",
     "Azure AD Join (now Entra Join) connects Windows devices directly to Azure AD without requiring on-premises AD or VPN. Methods: 1) During OOBE (Out of Box Experience): on a new or reset PC, at the 'Who owns this PC?' screen, select 'Organization'. Sign in with the user's Azure AD credentials. The device joins automatically if MDM auto-enrollment is configured. 2) From Settings: Settings → Accounts → Access work or school → Connect → select 'Join this device to Azure Active Directory'. Sign in with Azure AD credentials. 3) Via Autopilot: register the device's hardware hash in Intune → create an Autopilot deployment profile (user-driven or self-deploying). The device auto-joins on first boot and applies all Intune policies and apps. Prerequisites: Azure AD Premium P1 (for MDM auto-enrollment), Intune license, MDM auto-enrollment configured in Azure AD → Mobility (MDM and MAM). After joining: users sign in with Azure AD credentials (user@company.com). Device gets Intune policies and compliance checks. Conditional Access can enforce: MFA, compliant device, approved location. The device appears in Azure AD → Devices and Intune → Devices. Hybrid Azure AD Join is an alternative where the device joins both on-premises AD and Azure AD simultaneously—used during migration or when on-premises resources require domain membership."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot a computer that can't join an Active Directory domain?",
     "Common errors and solutions: 1) 'The specified domain does not exist or could not be contacted': DNS is the most common cause. Verify the computer's DNS points to the domain controller: 'nslookup domain.com' should return the DC's IP. Fix: set DNS to the DC's IP address. 2) 'The account already exists': a computer object with the same name exists in AD. Either delete/reset it in AD Users and Computers, or pre-stage the account in the correct OU. 3) 'Access denied': the user joining doesn't have permissions. By default, authenticated users can join up to 10 computers. Check with: 'Get-ADObject (Get-ADDomain).DistinguishedName -Properties ms-DS-MachineAccountQuota'. Alternatively, pre-create the computer object and grant the user permissions to it. 4) Network connectivity: ensure the computer can reach the DC on ports: TCP/UDP 53 (DNS), TCP 88 (Kerberos), TCP 135 (RPC), TCP 389 (LDAP), TCP 445 (SMB), TCP 636 (LDAPS). Test with: 'Test-NetConnection DC01 -Port 389'. 5) Time synchronization: Kerberos requires time difference less than 5 minutes. Check: 'w32tm /stripchart /computer:dc01.domain.com'. Fix: 'net time \\\\dc01 /set /y'. 6) Firewall blocking: temporarily disable the firewall to test, then create proper rules. 7) Secure channel broken (for re-joining): 'Test-ComputerSecureChannel -Repair -Credential (Get-Credential)'."),
    ("What is DISM and how is it used for Windows servicing and repair?",
     "DISM (Deployment Image Servicing and Management) is a command-line tool for servicing Windows images (online running OS or offline WIM/VHD files). Common repair commands: 1) Check image health: 'DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /CheckHealth' (quick check). 2) Scan for corruption: 'DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /ScanHealth' (thorough scan). 3) Repair corruption: 'DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth' (downloads repair files from Windows Update). If no internet: '/Source:D:\\Sources\\install.wim' to use a Windows ISO as the source. 4) Use with SFC: run DISM first to repair the component store, then 'sfc /scannow' to fix system files using the repaired store. Servicing uses: 5) Add drivers to an image: 'DISM /Image:C:\\mount /Add-Driver /Driver:C:\\drivers /Recurse'. 6) Add Windows features: 'DISM /Online /Enable-Feature /FeatureName:NetFx3 /Source:D:\\Sources\\sxs'. 7) Remove updates: 'DISM /Online /Remove-Package /PackageName:<package>'. 8) Capture a reference image: 'DISM /Capture-Image /ImageFile:C:\\custom.wim /CaptureDir:C:\\ /Name:\"Custom Windows\"'. 9) Clean up component store (free disk space): 'DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /StartComponentCleanup /ResetBase'. DISM is essential for IT pros managing Windows deployments and troubleshooting system file corruption."),
    ("How do I set up two-factor authentication for a Linux SSH server?",
     "Using Google Authenticator PAM module: 1) Install: 'apt install libpam-google-authenticator' (Debian/Ubuntu) or 'yum install google-authenticator' (RHEL/CentOS from EPEL). 2) As the user who will SSH in, run: 'google-authenticator'. Answer the prompts: time-based tokens (yes), update .google_authenticator file (yes), disallow multiple uses of same token (yes), increase time window (no for security), enable rate-limiting (yes). Scan the QR code with an authenticator app (Google Authenticator, Authy, Microsoft Authenticator). Save the emergency scratch codes securely. 3) Configure PAM: edit /etc/pam.d/sshd, add: 'auth required pam_google_authenticator.so' after @include common-auth. 4) Configure SSH: edit /etc/ssh/sshd_config, set: 'ChallengeResponseAuthentication yes' and 'AuthenticationMethods publickey,keyboard-interactive' (requires both SSH key AND TOTP code—most secure). Or 'AuthenticationMethods keyboard-interactive' for password + TOTP. 5) Restart SSH: 'systemctl restart sshd'. Test with a NEW terminal before closing the current session—if misconfigured, you could lock yourself out. Keep a console/out-of-band access method available."),
    ("What is a content delivery network (CDN) and how does it improve web performance?",
     "A CDN is a distributed network of servers (edge locations/PoPs—Points of Presence) placed geographically close to end users. Instead of every request going to your origin server (which might be in one location), the CDN serves content from the nearest edge location. How it works: 1) User requests your website. DNS resolves to the nearest CDN edge. 2) If the edge has the content cached, it serves it immediately (cache hit)—dramatically reducing latency. 3) If not cached (cache miss), the edge fetches from your origin server, serves it to the user, and caches it for future requests. Benefits: reduced latency (content served from nearby), reduced origin server load (CDN handles most requests), DDoS protection (CDN absorbs attack traffic across its global network), SSL/TLS offloading, automatic image optimization, and geographic redundancy. Types of content served: static (images, CSS, JavaScript, videos, downloads) and increasingly dynamic content. Major CDNs: Cloudflare (also provides DNS, WAF, DDoS protection), AWS CloudFront, Azure CDN, Akamai, Fastly. For IT support: CDN issues manifest as: stale content (cache not purging—purge the CDN cache), mixed content errors (HTTP resources on HTTPS pages), CORS issues, or SSL certificate mismatches. Check CDN headers (X-Cache, CF-Cache-Status) to diagnose."),
    ("How do I configure a Windows Server as a file server with DFS for multiple branch offices?",
     "DFS (Distributed File System) provides two key features: DFS Namespaces (DFS-N) and DFS Replication (DFS-R). DFS Namespaces creates a unified folder structure: users access \\\\company.com\\shares instead of \\\\server1\\finance or \\\\server2\\hr. If a server changes, you update the namespace target—users don't notice. Setup: 1) Install DFS roles: Server Manager → Add Roles → File and Storage Services → DFS Namespaces + DFS Replication. 2) Create namespace: DFS Management console → New Namespace. Choose domain-based (recommended—stored in AD, available to all domain members) or standalone. 3) Add folders to the namespace: each folder points to one or more folder targets (actual shared folders on servers). DFS Replication keeps folder targets synchronized across servers: 1) Create a replication group: DFS Management → Replication → New Replication Group. 2) Add member servers, select folders to replicate. 3) Configure topology: full mesh (all servers replicate to all others—best for few servers) or hub-and-spoke (central hub, branches sync with hub—best for many branch offices). 4) Set schedule and bandwidth: limit replication during business hours if WAN bandwidth is limited. 5) Set a primary member for initial sync. Monitor: DFS Management → Replication → Diagnostic Reports. Benefits for branch offices: local file access speeds, automatic failover if one server goes down, centralized management of a distributed file system."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot Microsoft Teams when it won't load or keeps showing a white screen?",
     "Progressive troubleshooting: 1) Clear Teams cache (most common fix): close Teams completely (check system tray). Delete: %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\Cache, %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\blob_storage, %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\databases, %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\GPUCache, %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\IndexedDB, %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\Local Storage, %AppData%\\Microsoft\\Teams\\tmp. Restart Teams. For new Teams (Teams 2.0): delete %LocalAppData%\\Packages\\MSTeams_* folder contents. 2) Check for client updates: Teams updates automatically but sometimes gets stuck. Download the latest installer from Microsoft and reinstall. 3) Clear credentials: Credential Manager → remove all entries for 'msteams' or 'microsoftteams'. 4) GPU hardware acceleration: Settings → General → disable 'GPU hardware acceleration' (can cause blank screens on some hardware). 5) Check proxy/firewall: Teams needs access to *.microsoft.com, *.microsoftonline.com, *.teams.microsoft.com, *.skype.com on ports 443 and 80. 6) Network: Teams requires specific endpoints. Test with Microsoft's connectivity test tool. 7) If using web Teams (teams.microsoft.com): clear browser cache, try incognito mode, try a different browser. 8) Check M365 Service Health for known issues."),
    ("What is the principle of defense in depth and how is it implemented in IT security?",
     "Defense in depth is a security strategy that uses multiple layers of protection so that if one layer fails, others still protect the system. Layers (from outside in): 1) Physical security: locked server rooms, security cameras, badge access, cable locks for laptops. 2) Perimeter security: firewalls (next-gen with IPS/IDS), DMZ for public-facing servers, DDoS protection, WAF for web applications. 3) Network security: network segmentation (VLANs), micro-segmentation, 802.1X network access control, network monitoring (IDS/IPS), encrypted tunnels (VPN). 4) Host security: endpoint protection (EDR), host-based firewall, OS hardening (CIS benchmarks), patch management, application whitelisting. 5) Application security: secure coding practices, input validation, web application firewall, regular vulnerability scanning and penetration testing. 6) Data security: encryption at rest (BitLocker, Azure Storage encryption) and in transit (TLS), data loss prevention (DLP), access controls, data classification. 7) Identity security: MFA, conditional access, privileged access management, zero trust (verify every access request regardless of location). 8) User awareness: security training, phishing simulations, clear security policies. No single layer is sufficient—the goal is that an attacker must bypass multiple controls, each making the attack harder and more detectable."),
    ("How do I manage and troubleshoot Windows services using PowerShell?",
     "Essential PowerShell commands for services: View services: 'Get-Service' (all services), 'Get-Service -Name Spooler' (specific service), 'Get-Service | Where Status -eq Stopped' (all stopped services), 'Get-Service -DisplayName \"*print*\"' (search by display name). Start/Stop/Restart: 'Start-Service Spooler', 'Stop-Service Spooler -Force' (force stops dependent services too), 'Restart-Service Spooler'. Change startup type: 'Set-Service -Name Spooler -StartupType Automatic', options: Automatic, Manual, Disabled, AutomaticDelayedStart. Check dependencies: 'Get-Service Spooler | Select -ExpandProperty DependentServices' and 'Get-Service Spooler | Select -ExpandProperty ServicesDependedOn'. Remote management: 'Get-Service -ComputerName server01 -Name Spooler', 'Invoke-Command -ComputerName server01 -ScriptBlock {Restart-Service Spooler}'. Troubleshooting a service that won't start: 'Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName=\"System\"; ProviderName=\"Service Control Manager\"} -MaxEvents 20'—shows service start/stop events and errors. Check the service's recovery options: services.msc → service → Properties → Recovery tab (restart service, run a program, or restart the computer on failure). For services running as a specific account, verify the account password hasn't changed and has 'Log on as a service' right."),
    ("What are the key differences between IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS cloud service models?",
     "The three main cloud service models differ in what the provider manages vs. what you manage: IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service): The provider gives you virtual machines, storage, and networking. You manage: OS, runtime, middleware, applications, data. Provider manages: physical hardware, virtualization, networking infrastructure. Examples: AWS EC2, Azure VMs, Google Compute Engine. Use when: you need full control over the OS and middleware, migrating existing applications ('lift and shift'), running custom software. PaaS (Platform as a Service): The provider gives you a platform to deploy applications. You manage: applications and data. Provider manages: OS, runtime, middleware, infrastructure. Examples: Azure App Service, AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Google App Engine, Heroku. Use when: developing web applications, you want to focus on code not infrastructure, need auto-scaling without managing servers. SaaS (Software as a Service): The provider gives you complete applications. You manage: user accounts, data, configuration. Provider manages: everything else. Examples: Microsoft 365, Salesforce, Google Workspace, Dropbox, Zoom. Use when: you need standard business applications without managing any infrastructure. As you move from IaaS to SaaS, you have less control but less management overhead—choose based on your needs for customization vs. simplicity."),
    ("How do I set up and configure a jump server (bastion host) for secure remote access?",
     "A jump server acts as a single controlled access point to your internal network from the internet. All remote administration traffic goes through it, creating a single point for monitoring, logging, and access control. Setup: 1) Deploy a hardened server in a DMZ or dedicated subnet. Windows Server (with RD Gateway role) or Linux (SSH). 2) Harden aggressively: remove all unnecessary roles/features/services. Apply CIS benchmarks. Enable full audit logging. Install EDR. Enable host-based firewall—allow only RDP (3389) or SSH (22) inbound from authorized external IPs. 3) Access control: require MFA for all logins. Limit user accounts to authorized administrators only. Use named accounts (no shared credentials). Implement session recording for accountability. 4) Network rules: the jump server should be the ONLY path from external to internal management interfaces. Firewall rules: internet → jump server (RDP/SSH), jump server → internal servers (RDP/SSH/management ports), deny direct internet-to-internal management traffic. 5) For Windows: install RD Gateway role—it tunnels RDP over HTTPS (443), works through firewalls, and integrates with NPS for RADIUS authentication and MFA. 6) For cloud: use Azure Bastion (managed jump server, no public IPs on VMs) or AWS Systems Manager Session Manager (no jump server needed, logs all sessions). 7) Monitor: alert on all logins, failed attempts, and unusual hours of access."),
    ("How do I configure Network Level Authentication (NLA) for Remote Desktop and why is it important?",
     "NLA requires users to authenticate BEFORE a remote desktop session is established, rather than after. Without NLA, the server presents the login screen to anyone who connects—consuming resources and exposing the server to pre-authentication attacks (like BlueKeep). With NLA, credentials are verified via CredSSP before the session starts, so unauthenticated users never see the login screen. Enable NLA: 1) Via System Properties: right-click This PC → Properties → Remote settings → check 'Allow connections only from computers running Remote Desktop with Network Level Authentication'. 2) Via GPO: Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Remote Desktop Services → Session Host → Security → 'Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication' = Enabled. 3) Via PowerShell: 'Set-ItemProperty -Path \"HKLM:\\System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\\WinStations\\RDP-Tcp\" -Name UserAuthentication -Value 1'. Verify: 'Get-ItemProperty ... -Name UserAuthentication' should return 1. Considerations: NLA requires the client to support CredSSP (all modern Windows clients do). For domain-joined machines, Kerberos handles authentication. For non-domain machines, the user needs to enter domain\\username. Smart card authentication works with NLA. Ensure the Remote Desktop Users group includes authorized users."),
    ("What are the best practices for managing software licenses in an enterprise?",
     "License management prevents both legal liability (under-licensing fines can be millions) and wasted spend (over-licensing). Best practices: 1) Maintain a Software Asset Management (SAM) inventory: track all purchased licenses (type, quantity, terms, expiration, cost) and all installed software (discovery tools: SCCM, PDQ Inventory, Lansweeper, or Intune). 2) Understand license types: per-device, per-user, per-core, per-processor, subscription, perpetual, concurrent. Microsoft licensing: per-user vs. per-device CALs, M365 E3 vs. E5 differences. 3) Regular reconciliation: compare purchased licenses against actual installations quarterly. Identify: unlicensed installations (compliance risk), unused licenses (cost savings), underutilized subscriptions (downgrade opportunities). 4) Centralize procurement: all software purchases go through IT/procurement to prevent shadow IT and duplicate purchases. 5) Use a license management tool: ServiceNow SAM, Snow License Manager, Flexera. 6) Maintain documentation: purchase orders, license agreements, certificates, invoices. Keep for 7+ years for audit purposes. 7) Prepare for audits: Microsoft, Oracle, Adobe, and others regularly audit enterprises. Having accurate records prevents fines. 8) Consider true-up opportunities: Enterprise Agreements have annual true-ups where you can adjust license counts. 9) Automate reclamation: detect unused licenses (no login for 90 days) and reassign them."),
    ("How do I set up alerts and dashboards in Azure Monitor for cloud resources?",
     "Azure Monitor provides comprehensive monitoring for Azure resources. Setup: 1) Metrics alerts: Azure Portal → Monitor → Alerts → Create alert rule. Select target resource (VM, App Service, SQL Database). Define condition: metric (CPU percentage, available memory, disk IOPS), operator (greater than), threshold (90%), aggregation (average over 5 minutes). Configure actions: email, SMS, Azure Function, Logic App, webhook, ITSM connector. Severity: 0 (Critical) to 4 (Verbose). 2) Log alerts (based on Log Analytics queries): define a KQL query like 'Heartbeat | where TimeGenerated > ago(5m) | summarize count() by Computer | where count_ < 1' (detects servers that stopped sending heartbeats). 3) Activity log alerts: trigger on Azure operations (VM deleted, role assignment changed). 4) Dashboards: Azure Portal → Dashboard → New. Add tiles: drag metrics charts, log query visualizations, resource health indicators. Pin from any Azure Monitor chart: click 'Pin to dashboard'. Share dashboards with team members. 5) Workbooks: more advanced interactive reports. Monitor → Workbooks → start from templates (VM Insights, Network Insights) or create custom. 6) Enable VM Insights: Monitor → Virtual Machines → enable monitoring. Automatically installs agents and provides performance, dependency maps. Best practice: start with the built-in recommended alerts for each resource type, then customize based on your SLAs and operational requirements."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot certificate errors in a web browser?",
     "Common certificate errors and fixes: 1) 'Your connection is not private' / NET::ERR_CERT_AUTHORITY_INVALID: the certificate isn't signed by a trusted CA. Causes: self-signed certificate (install the CA cert in the browser or OS trust store), expired intermediate CA (the server isn't sending the full certificate chain—fix the server configuration to include intermediate certs), corporate SSL inspection (install the corporate root CA certificate). 2) ERR_CERT_DATE_INVALID: certificate is expired or not yet valid. Check: is the server's certificate expired? Renew it. Is the computer's date/time wrong? Fix it—certificates validate against the local clock. 3) ERR_CERT_COMMON_NAME_INVALID: the domain in the URL doesn't match the certificate's Common Name or Subject Alternative Names (SANs). Causes: accessing via IP instead of hostname, accessing a different domain, certificate doesn't include all needed domain variations (www vs. non-www). Fix: get a certificate with the correct SANs. 4) Mixed content warnings: the page loads over HTTPS but includes resources (images, scripts) over HTTP. Fix: update all resource URLs to HTTPS. 5) HSTS errors (cannot bypass): the site previously sent a Strict-Transport-Security header. The browser refuses insecure connections and won't let you proceed. Clear HSTS settings: chrome://net-internals/#hsts → delete the domain."),
    ("What is Windows Autopilot and how does it simplify device provisioning?",
     "Windows Autopilot is a cloud-based service that automates setting up new Windows devices. Instead of IT manually imaging each device, the manufacturer or reseller registers the device's hardware hash with your Azure AD tenant. When a user receives a new device and turns it on: 1) The device connects to the internet during OOBE. 2) It contacts the Autopilot service, which identifies the device and applies your organization's Autopilot profile. 3) The user signs in with their Azure AD credentials. 4) The device joins Azure AD, enrolls in Intune, and receives all configured policies, apps, and settings automatically. Deployment modes: User-driven—the user completes the OOBE with their credentials. Best for standard users. Self-deploying—no user interaction needed. The device auto-configures as a shared device or kiosk. Requires TPM 2.0. Pre-provisioned (White Glove)—IT or the partner pre-provisions the device (installs apps, applies policies), then ships to the user who just signs in. Benefits: no custom images to maintain (uses the OEM-installed Windows), zero-touch deployment for remote workers (ship directly from manufacturer to employee), consistent experience regardless of hardware vendor. Prerequisites: Azure AD Premium, Intune, and hardware hash registration (via OEM, partner, or manual: 'Get-WindowsAutopilotInfo -OutputFile C:\\autopilot.csv')."),
    ("How do I configure log forwarding from Linux servers to a centralized syslog server?",
     "Using rsyslog (most common on modern Linux): On the Syslog server: 1) Edit /etc/rsyslog.conf. Uncomment the UDP and/or TCP listener lines: 'module(load=\"imudp\") input(type=\"imudp\" port=\"514\")' for UDP, 'module(load=\"imtcp\") input(type=\"imtcp\" port=\"514\")' for TCP (more reliable). 2) Create a template for organizing logs by client: 'template(name=\"RemoteLogs\" type=\"string\" string=\"/var/log/remote/%HOSTNAME%/%PROGRAMNAME%.log\")' and 'if $fromhost-ip != \"127.0.0.1\" then -?RemoteLogs & stop'. 3) Open firewall: 'ufw allow 514/tcp' and 'ufw allow 514/udp', or 'firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=514/tcp'. 4) Restart rsyslog: 'systemctl restart rsyslog'. On each client: 1) Edit /etc/rsyslog.conf, add at the end: '*.* @@syslog-server.company.com:514' (@@=TCP, @=UDP). Or for specific facilities: 'auth,authpriv.* @@syslog-server:514' (forward only authentication logs). 2) Restart rsyslog. Verify: check /var/log/remote/<hostname>/ on the server. Best practices: use TCP for reliability (UDP can drop messages under load). Enable TLS encryption for syslog (rsyslog supports TLS via the gtls module). Set log rotation on the central server. For large environments, consider a log aggregator like Fluentd, Filebeat, or Vector that can forward to ELK/Splunk."),
    ("What is a VLAN and why would I segment a network into VLANs?",
     "A VLAN (Virtual Local Area Network) is a logical grouping of network devices that behave as if they're on the same physical network, even if they're connected to different switches. Devices in the same VLAN can communicate at Layer 2 (switching); devices in different VLANs need a router or Layer 3 switch to communicate. Why segment: 1) Security—isolate sensitive systems (finance, HR, servers) from general user traffic. A compromised workstation in the user VLAN can't directly access the server VLAN. 2) Performance—broadcast traffic is contained within the VLAN. Without VLANs, a broadcast reaches every device on the network. With VLANs, it only reaches devices in that VLAN, reducing unnecessary traffic. 3) Compliance—PCI DSS requires cardholder data environment to be segmented from the rest of the network. HIPAA similarly requires isolation of systems with PHI. 4) Organization—logical grouping by department, function, or security level regardless of physical location. Common VLAN design: VLAN 10 = Management, VLAN 20 = Servers, VLAN 30 = User workstations, VLAN 40 = VoIP phones, VLAN 50 = Guest Wi-Fi, VLAN 100 = IoT/printers. Inter-VLAN routing is done by a Layer 3 switch or firewall, which also applies ACLs to control which VLANs can communicate and on which ports."),
    ("How do I use Windows System Restore to recover from a problematic change?",
     "System Restore creates snapshots (restore points) of the system state: registry, system files, installed programs, and driver configurations. It does NOT affect personal files. Using it: 1) From Windows: search 'Create a restore point' → System Restore → Next → select a restore point from the list (sorted by date). Click 'Scan for affected programs' to see what will be removed or restored. Click Next → Finish. The computer restarts. 2) From Safe Mode: if Windows won't start normally, boot into Safe Mode (hold Shift while clicking Restart → Troubleshoot → Advanced options → System Restore). 3) From WinRE (Windows Recovery Environment): if Safe Mode doesn't work, boot from installation media or force WinRE (hard reboot 3 times) → Troubleshoot → Advanced options → System Restore. Configuration: System Properties → System Protection → select drive → Configure → Turn on system protection → set Max Usage (5-10% of disk is usually sufficient). Restore points are created automatically: before Windows Updates, driver installations, and software installations. Manual: click 'Create' in System Protection. Important: System Restore doesn't replace backups—it can't recover from hardware failure or ransomware that encrypts restore points. BitLocker-encrypted drives may require the recovery key after a restore."),
    ("How do I configure and manage Azure AD Multi-Factor Authentication?",
     "Azure AD MFA setup: 1) Per-user MFA (legacy): Azure Portal → Azure AD → Users → Per-user MFA. Enable/Enforce for individual users. Three states: Disabled (MFA not required), Enabled (user prompted to set up MFA at next login), Enforced (MFA is required, user has completed registration). 2) Conditional Access (recommended): Azure Portal → Azure AD → Security → Conditional Access → New policy. Much more flexible: apply MFA based on conditions (all users except break-glass accounts, all cloud apps, require MFA). This is the modern approach. 3) Security Defaults (simplest): Azure AD → Properties → Manage Security defaults → Yes. Enables MFA for all users with Microsoft Authenticator. Good for small organizations without Azure AD Premium. Authentication methods: Microsoft Authenticator app (push notification—most secure and user-friendly), FIDO2 security keys (phishing-resistant, best for high-security), SMS/voice call (least secure—vulnerable to SIM swap attacks, use only as backup), OATH hardware tokens, Temporary Access Pass (for onboarding). Configure methods: Azure AD → Security → Authentication methods. Best practices: enforce Authenticator app over SMS, enable number matching to prevent MFA fatigue attacks, require MFA for all admin accounts without exception, maintain 2+ break-glass accounts excluded from MFA policies, monitor MFA registration: Azure AD → Usage & Insights → Authentication Methods activity."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot a computer that won't boot past the BIOS/UEFI screen?",
     "The system is POST-ing (showing BIOS) but not loading the OS. Troubleshooting: 1) Check boot order: enter BIOS/UEFI (usually F2, F12, Del, or Esc during startup). Verify the boot drive is listed and prioritized. If the drive isn't listed, it may not be detected. 2) Drive not detected: check physical connections—SATA cable, power cable. Try a different SATA port. For NVMe: check if the M.2 slot supports NVMe (some only support SATA). Try the drive in another machine to verify it works. 3) Secure Boot issues: if you installed Linux or changed hardware, Secure Boot may prevent booting. Temporarily disable Secure Boot in BIOS to test. 4) Boot mode mismatch: if the OS was installed in UEFI mode, the BIOS must boot in UEFI mode (not Legacy/CSM). Check: does the disk have a GPT partition table (UEFI) or MBR (Legacy)? 5) Corrupted bootloader: boot from Windows installation USB → Repair your computer → Command Prompt → 'bootrec /fixboot', 'bootrec /fixmbr', 'bootrec /rebuildbcd'. For UEFI: 'bcdboot C:\\Windows /s S: /f UEFI' (where S: is the EFI partition). 6) Hardware failure: try booting from a USB drive (Windows installer or Linux live USB). If that works, the hard drive may be failing—check with CrystalDiskInfo or manufacturer diagnostics. 7) RAM issues: remove one stick at a time to test. Reseat RAM modules. 8) CMOS battery: a dead battery can cause BIOS settings to reset, including boot order."),
    ("What is PXE boot and how is it used for network-based OS deployment?",
     "PXE (Preboot Execution Environment) allows computers to boot from the network, loading an OS installer or image from a server instead of local media. How it works: 1) Computer powers on with PXE boot enabled in BIOS (usually triggered by pressing F12 for network boot). 2) The network adapter broadcasts a DHCP discover with PXE options (Option 66—boot server, Option 67—boot filename). 3) A DHCP server responds with an IP address and the PXE boot server information. 4) The computer downloads a small boot program (pxelinux.0, wimboot, or iPXE) from the TFTP server. 5) The boot program loads a boot menu or directly starts the OS installer/imaging process. Enterprise deployment: WDS (Windows Deployment Services)—Microsoft's PXE server. Install the WDS role, add boot images (boot.wim from Windows ISO) and install images (install.wim). Configure DHCP options to point to WDS. SCCM/MECM—uses PXE-enabled distribution points. More powerful: task sequences automate the entire OS deployment process. MDT—uses WDS for PXE, adds task sequences and driver management. Configuration: in BIOS, enable PXE boot and set network boot in the boot order (usually after local disk so it only PXE boots when intended, or use F12 for on-demand network boot). Firewall: allow DHCP (UDP 67/68), TFTP (UDP 69), and multicast if using it."),
    ("How do I manage local administrator accounts securely with LAPS?",
     "LAPS (Local Administrator Password Solution) automatically manages and rotates the local admin password on domain-joined devices, storing it securely in Active Directory. Each computer gets a unique, randomly generated password. Setup (Legacy LAPS): 1) Download LAPS from Microsoft. 2) Extend the AD schema: run 'Update-AdmPwdADSchema' from an elevated PowerShell (requires Schema Admin rights). This adds two attributes to computer objects: ms-Mcs-AdmPwd (the password) and ms-Mcs-AdmPwdExpirationTime. 3) Set permissions: 'Set-AdmPwdComputerSelfPermission -OrgUnit \"OU=Workstations,DC=company,DC=com\"'—allows computers to update their own password. 'Set-AdmPwdReadPasswordPermission -OrgUnit ... -AllowedPrincipals \"IT Helpdesk\"'—allows helpdesk to read passwords. 4) Configure GPO: Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → LAPS. Enable 'Enable local admin password management'. Set password complexity and length (14+ characters, complexity enabled). Set password age (30 days). 5) Deploy the LAPS client (CSE—Client Side Extension) via GPO software installation, SCCM, or Intune. Windows LAPS (built into Windows 11 and Server 2022+): native, no additional install needed. Supports Azure AD in addition to on-premises AD. Passwords can be stored in Azure AD and viewed in the Entra admin center. Retrieving passwords: LAPS UI tool, PowerShell: 'Get-AdmPwdPassword -ComputerName PC001', or Active Directory Users and Computers (LAPS tab on the computer object)."),
    ("How do I troubleshoot Group Policy Preferences drive mapping that isn't working?",
     "GPP drive maps fail silently, making troubleshooting tricky. Steps: 1) Verify the GPO applies: 'gpresult /H report.html'. Open the report and look under User Configuration → Group Policy Preferences → Drive Maps. If it shows 'Denied' or isn't listed, check GPO link, security filtering, and WMI filters. 2) Check item-level targeting: if the drive map uses item-level targeting (e.g., 'apply only to users in the Finance security group'), verify the user meets ALL conditions. Common mistake: targeting by OU path when users have moved to a different OU. 3) Check the action type: Create (only creates if the mapping doesn't exist), Delete (removes the mapping), Replace (deletes then creates), Update (modifies existing or creates if missing). Use 'Update' for most scenarios—it's the most flexible. 4) Check credentials: GPP used to support stored credentials, but this was disabled due to the ms14-025 vulnerability (credentials were easily decrypted). If the mapping requires different credentials, it won't work with GPP—use a logon script instead. 5) Drive letter conflict: if the letter is already in use by another mapping, the preference fails. 6) Check Event Viewer: Applications and Services Logs → Microsoft → Windows → GroupPolicy → Operational. Filter for Event IDs 4016 (success) and 4098 (failure)—the failure event usually includes the specific error code and reason. 7) Run 'gpupdate /force' and log off/log on (drive maps apply at logon, not during gpupdate)."),
    ("What is Windows Server Update Services (WSUS) maintenance and why is it important?",
     "WSUS requires regular maintenance or it becomes slow and unreliable. Key maintenance tasks: 1) Server Cleanup Wizard: WSUS Console → Options → Server Cleanup Wizard. Remove: unneeded updates (superseded, expired, declined), computers that haven't contacted the server in 30+ days, unneeded update files, unneeded update revisions. Run monthly. 2) Decline superseded updates: updates that have been replaced by newer ones. Either manually or with PowerShell: 'Get-WsusUpdate -Approval AnyExceptDeclined -Status Any | Where {$_.Update.IsSuperseded} | Deny-WsusUpdate'. 3) Re-index the WSUS database: the WID (Windows Internal Database) or SQL database gets fragmented. Run the Microsoft-provided maintenance SQL script monthly. This significantly improves console responsiveness. 4) IIS Application Pool recycling: WSUS Application Pool → Advanced Settings → Private Memory Limit. Set to 0 (unlimited) or a high value (8GB+) to prevent crashes during sync. Increase the queue length to 25000. 5) Disk space: WSUS can consume hundreds of GB. Only sync products and categories you actually use. Remove update files for declined updates. 6) Reboot the WSUS server monthly after maintenance. 7) Monitor sync status: check that synchronization with Microsoft Update is completing successfully. Check the SyncLog.log and wsyncmgr.log for errors. Without maintenance, common symptoms include: WSUS console taking minutes to load or timing out, sync failures, high CPU/memory/disk usage on the server, and clients reporting they can't reach the update server."),
    ("How do I set up Windows Remote Management (WinRM) for enterprise management?",
     "WinRM is the Microsoft implementation of WS-Management protocol, used by PowerShell Remoting, Server Manager, and many management tools. Setup: 1) Enable WinRM: 'winrm quickconfig' or via GPO: Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Windows Remote Management → WinRM Service → 'Allow remote server management through WinRM' = Enabled. Set IPv4/IPv6 filters to * or specific subnets. 2) GPO for automatic setup (recommended for enterprise): Also enable: 'Allow Basic authentication' = Disabled (use Kerberos), 'Allow CredSSP authentication' = as needed. Windows Firewall: 'Windows Remote Management (HTTP-In)' = Enabled for Domain profile. 3) WinRM listener configuration: by default, HTTP on port 5985. For HTTPS (port 5986), install a server certificate and create an HTTPS listener: 'winrm create winrm/config/Listener?Address=*+Transport=HTTPS @{Hostname=\"server.company.com\"; CertificateThumbprint=\"<thumbprint>\"}'. 4) Trusted Hosts (for non-domain environments): 'Set-Item WSMan:\\localhost\\Client\\TrustedHosts -Value \"192.168.1.*\"'. 5) Testing: 'Test-WSMan -ComputerName server01' (basic test), 'Enter-PSSession -ComputerName server01' (full test). 6) Security: use HTTPS in production, restrict WinRM access to management subnets via firewall, use JEA (Just Enough Administration) to limit what remote users can do, and log all WinRM sessions via PowerShell transcription logging."),
]

